.TH iouringsnoop 8  "2024-10-01" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
iouringsnoop \- Trace io_uring request latency. Uses Linux eBPF/bcc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iouringsnoop [\-h] [\-p PID] [\-c COMM] [\-m MIN_US] [\-r CTX] [\-e] [\-T] [\-v] [duration]
.SH DESCRIPTION
iouringsnoop traces Linux io_uring requests from submission to completion,
reporting per-request latency in microseconds alongside the operation
opcode, PID, process name, and CQE result code.

io_uring is the Linux kernel's high-performance asynchronous I/O interface
(introduced in Linux 5.1).  Traditional tools such as
.BR biosnoop (8),
.BR fileslower (8),
and
.BR syscount (8)
do not observe io_uring traffic because io_uring bypasses the standard VFS
system call path.  iouringsnoop fills this gap.

This tool attaches to the kernel tracepoints:
.IP \(bu 2
.B io_uring:io_uring_submit_req
(available since Linux 5.17)
.IP \(bu 2
.B io_uring:io_uring_complete
(available since Linux 5.10)

State (submission timestamp and PID/comm) is stored in a
.B BPF_MAP_TYPE_HASH
keyed by the
.I io_kiocb
request pointer.  On completion the entry is deleted to prevent map leaks.

When kernel BTF support is present (CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y), the tool
uses
.B tp_btf
programs (type-safe tracepoint programs).  On older kernels without full
BTF support for these tracepoints, it falls back automatically to
.B raw_tp
programs.

Since this uses BPF, only the root user can use this tool.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.TP
CONFIG_IO_URING=y
(Linux 5.1 or later)
.TP
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y
Recommended for CO-RE and tp_btf support.  raw_tp fallback works
without this option.
.TP
Linux kernel >= 5.17
Required for the
.I io_uring_submit_req
tracepoint.  On kernels 5.10\-5.16 only
.I io_uring_complete
is available; iouringsnoop will report an error on those kernels.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-h
Print usage message.
.TP
\-p PID
Trace this process ID only (filtered in-kernel).
.TP
\-c COMM
Trace processes whose name starts with COMM (filtered in-kernel).
.TP
\-m MIN_US
Only report requests whose latency exceeds MIN_US microseconds.
.TP
\-r CTX
Trace only the io_ring_ctx at the given hexadecimal address.  Useful when
a process has multiple rings open.
.TP
\-e
Report only requests that completed with a negative result (errors).
.TP
\-T
Include a wall-clock TIME column in the output.
.TP
\-v
Verbose debug output (libbpf log).
.TP
duration
Trace for this many seconds, then exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Trace all io_uring activity system-wide:
.B # iouringsnoop
.TP
Trace PID 12345 only:
.B # iouringsnoop \-p 12345
.TP
Trace a process named 'fio':
.B # iouringsnoop \-c fio
.TP
Show only slow requests (latency > 1 ms = 1000 µs):
.B # iouringsnoop \-m 1000
.TP
Show only failed requests:
.B # iouringsnoop \-e
.TP
Trace for 10 seconds with timestamps:
.B # iouringsnoop \-T 10
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
TIME
Wall-clock timestamp (only with \-T flag).
.TP
COMM
Name of the process that submitted the request.
.TP
PID
Process ID of the submitter.
.TP
OPCODE
Human-readable io_uring opcode name.  Examples:
.BR READV ,
.BR WRITEV ,
.BR ACCEPT ,
.BR CONNECT ,
.BR SEND ,
.BR RECV ,
.BR SPLICE ,
.BR POLL_ADD ,
.BR TIMEOUT .
.TP
LAT(us)
Submission-to-completion latency in microseconds.
.TP
RET
CQE result code.  Negative values indicate errors (errno negated).
Positive values are typically byte counts or file descriptors.
.SH OVERHEAD
iouringsnoop attaches to two kernel tracepoints and performs one hash map
lookup and deletion per request completion.  At moderate io_uring rates
(up to tens of thousands of requests per second) the overhead is expected
to be well under 1 % CPU.  At very high rates (hundreds of thousands per
second) consider using the \-m option to filter slow requests in-kernel
and reduce the volume of perf buffer events.
.SH SOURCE
This is from bcc.
.IP
https://github.com/iovisor/bcc
.PP
Also look in the bcc distribution for a companion _example.txt file
containing example usage, output, and commentary for this tool.
.SH OS
Linux
.SH STABILITY
Unstable \- in development.
.SH AUTHOR
Added via BCC community contribution.
